00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.040
I
thought it might be interesting to-- I'm not

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:32.209
going to focus so much on directly the research
that I'm doing and the

00:01:32.209 --> 00:01:38.969
details of some of the things that we're doing
here, with respect to BD2K.

00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:45.109
But I thought it would be useful to maybe
talk a little more generally about the role

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:51.340
of data in scientific discovery, and some
trends that are emerging with

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.079
how to manage data in the process of scientific
discovery.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:59.669
Particularly, a lot of it's driven by BD2K
and big data.

00:01:59.669 --> 00:02:05.320
And my experience has been that a lot of scientists
who are

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:11.371
doing even data analysis, data generation,
doing lots of data-driven science, there's

00:02:11.371 --> 00:02:16.280
been a lot of work and a of
thinking in how one should go about managing

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.820
that process.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:23.780
And I realize that, especially people who
are more

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:31.280
domain-oriented along the biomedical discovery
tend not to be aware of some of the thinking

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.760
and ideas that have
gone on.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:40.780
And so I thought I would spend some time reviewing
background and trends of what's happening

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.180
in data
management that, I think, are going to be

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:50.230
especially relevant to this audience in kind
of your daily practice.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:59.770
So what I hope to get out of this talk is--
I apologize if people in the audience actually

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.210
know this stuff already.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.511
But
what I would hope we get out of this is some

00:03:04.511 --> 00:03:09.600
recognition as to how to adapt your data management,
data

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:16.000
collection practices, especially driven by
data-oriented discovery and big data, and

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.350
how you might take away
some practical things, or at least some ways

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:27.820
to think about what you're doing that I think
it's important.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:29.320
So that is
our goal.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.740
And then I will show you some of the work
we've done that bring some of these ideas

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:34.930
to practice.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.600
So with
that as kind of the goal with what I'd like

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.540
to achieve, let's proceed forward as following.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.230
So I'd like to start actually with maybe an
existential question.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.190
Take a step back.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:52.800
What does it mean to have a
scientific result?

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.069
And maybe this is obvious.

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:57.510
But it's probably worth thinking a little
bit about.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:04.209
So at a higher level, a scientific result
is something that other people know about.

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:09.680
So this is if a tree falls in the
forest that nobody knows about it, does it

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:10.700
actually fall?

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.020
So we are concerned about communication, sharing
the

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.690
results.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:23.110
But more importantly, it's important that
others are able to validate that result.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:28.400
And we can break that down into three things.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.710
One is that we need to be able to reproduce
the method that was

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:39.509
used to create that result and achieve the
same answer so that we might be able to, in

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:43.139
some sense, repeat what
was being done.

00:04:43.139 --> 00:04:49.349
We would also ideally like to cross-validate,
which is we would like to get the same result

00:04:49.349 --> 00:04:50.379
using a
different method.

00:04:50.379 --> 00:04:56.319
And that way, we know what we've determined
is actually true in some sense.

00:04:56.319 --> 00:05:03.069
And then, ultimately, the result
should be something that we can reuse to create

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:08.039
a new method, and as a foundation for achieving
new scientific

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:09.379
results.

00:05:09.379 --> 00:05:16.199
So hopefully, these observations should be
obvious to you.

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:23.290
And here's a nice quote from somebody who
was involved in the theory of science, which

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:28.629
is a non-reproducible single occurrences are
of no significance to

00:05:28.629 --> 00:05:29.629
science.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.160
So I want you to take this to heart.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:39.160
If you produce a result, and it's not reproducible,
and it's a single occurrence,

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:42.060
this is scientifically irrelevant.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.569
So we should all be saying, well, of course.

00:05:45.569 --> 00:05:49.650
If we're doing science, the whole
purpose of science is to let people know what

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:53.759
we've done, and allow them to reproduce our
results.

00:05:53.759 --> 00:05:57.419
So why are we even having this conversation?

00:05:57.419 --> 00:06:01.930
Well, it turns out that even though this is
obvious, we're not

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.280
achieving this goal.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:10.919
So here are some results from a science paper--
sorry, nature paper-- that was published in

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:11.919
2016.

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:14.710
And you actually don't have to go very far.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.860
These things are showing up in the popular
literature.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:26.310
There's articles on NPR and the BBC, and so
on, about the reproducibility of scientific

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.979
results.

00:06:28.979 --> 00:06:34.330
And there's been
studies that show that one in 10 papers, the

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:37.800
results of one in 10 papers can be reproduced.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:44.699
So in May 2016, there
was a little article in Nature where they

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:53.300
went off and surveyed 1,500 researchers.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:56.960
And here's the direct quote from the Nature
paper.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:00.539
More than 70% of researchers have tried and
failed to

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:02.660
reproduce other scientist's experiments.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:08.469
And, more tellingly, this is really damning,
more than half were able to

00:07:08.469 --> 00:07:10.289
reproduce their own experiments.

00:07:10.289 --> 00:07:16.309
So even though the previous slide should be,
well, of course, that's obvious,

00:07:16.309 --> 00:07:20.199
when we look at what's going on in practice,
that's not what's happening.

00:07:20.199 --> 00:07:23.699
So not only can't we reproduce other people's
results.

00:07:23.699 --> 00:07:27.180
We cannot even reproduce our own results.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.110
So let's look at
a breakdown.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:37.020
So this is-- in Nature, the question is phrased
is, is there a crisis?

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:45.880
And what we see is that, depending on the
degree of crisis, 80% of the scientists surveyed

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.080
said there is a crisis.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:51.249
Actually, this is 90% said there's a crisis
in

00:07:51.249 --> 00:07:52.249
reproducibility.

00:07:52.249 --> 00:07:58.060
Over half of scientists surveyed said there's
a significant crisis.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:03.669
Those are pretty dramatic words to say there's
a crisis.

00:08:03.669 --> 00:08:10.369
And if we were to look at, again from the
survey, they

00:08:10.369 --> 00:08:16.259
asked, what is things that contribute to this
crisis?

00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:20.599
And what we see is that there's a lot of issues
that contribute.

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:27.319
And so we can't put our fingers on any one
thing.

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:33.690
So it goes everything from bad luck, to fraud,
to cherry picking

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:34.690
your data.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:38.310
But I think there's kind of two things here
that I'd like to focus on that are especially

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:40.570
relevant to be
BD2K.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:45.410
So one is the methods, or code, are unavailable.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:51.460
So we can't reproduce, because we don't have
the documentation of the methods or code in

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:54.310
a meaningful way.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:09:00.120
And then, secondly, lack of availability of
data, and I'm going to interpret this a little

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.140
more broadly than just raw
data.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:06.880
It can be just general lack of access to data.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:14.470
And actually these are two pretty significant
factors showing up kind of at the 80% level,

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:19.680
in terms of the results of
the reporting of this survey.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.300
And so, again, there's a lot of reasons why
we can't reproduce things.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:30.670
I was a little
surprised to learn that major source of lack

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:35.820
of reproducibility of biomedical results is
bad anti-bodies.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.410
They just don't work.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:46.260
And as a result of that, the NIH is requiring
anti-body [INAUDIBLE] validation procedures

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.670
to
be included as part of proposals that are

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:51.540
being submitted.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:57.440
But we're not necessarily talking about wet
lab

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.940
experiments here.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:07.690
How are these things, the notion of reproducibility,
relevant to us or in the BD2K community?

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:16.580
Well, I think what we need to think of is
that since we are, in many cases, analyzing

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:22.600
data, or we're doing things
computationally, really what we're doing is

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.620
we are conducting experiments with data.

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:32.190
And we need to think about
the same kind of experimental rigor that takes

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:35.670
place if we were sitting in a bench, or running
a clinical trial.

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:39.810
That
same kind of rigor, and experimental design,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:44.260
and documentation needs to take place when
we think about the

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:50.250
generation, consumption, and analysis of our
data.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:57.970
And I would argue that as we move to an increasingly
computational-based environment, and as we're

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:03.240
being
overwhelmed by data and new analytic methods,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.970
which is the underpinning of the whole idea
behind BD2K, the

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:18.040
role of data in either the reproducibility,
or lack of reproducibility, in our experiment

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.640
is only going to get more
important.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:25.160
So let's try to break this down a little bit,
and think about, what does it mean to have

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.130
reproducibility in data?

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:30.160
What
kinds of characteristics would you like?

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:34.440
What can we do to enhance our reproducibility
around data?

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:42.600
So you can think about this in kind of splitting
it up into-- I've got two main issues of points

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:45.970
that I think are worth
thinking about.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:54.870
One is at any level, data is only going to
be useful, just having the ones and zeros

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:59.850
for the numbers,
in and of itself is essentially of no use.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:05.010
And if I give you a pile full of numbers,
those are essentially meaningless to

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:06.010
me.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:12.760
And the only way that there is going to be
meaning or useful, the data will have utility,

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:18.690
is if I can figure out some
way, kind of where the data is so I can get

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:25.500
my hands on the numbers, what it is, what
these numbers represent,

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.240
and then ultimately how it was created.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:34.190
So if you think about it, only if I know how
to get my hands on it, what it is

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:41.130
that I'm trying to get my hands on, and how
it was produced, then if I don't have that,

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:45.730
then this whole notion of
reproducibility is really a nonstarter.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:50.910
So we need to think-- and I'm sure, again,
if we think about it maybe we're all doing

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:54.240
some of this in our daily
practice.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:13:00.430
But I think there's value in thinking about
these issues a little more explicitly, and

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:06.370
thinking about how does
this structure the way we set up our computational

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.580
laboratory?

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.000
The second thing I'd like to comment on is
when

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:19.770
we think about reproducibility of data, I
think there is a tendency to focus on publication

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:25.380
as the main artifact for
which there has to be reproducibility.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:29.070
So we think about, when we send that thing
off to Nature, then we have to document what

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:31.890
we've been doing so
our protocol could be reproduced.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:37.030
Then, computationally, I think we often think
that way.

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.150
And in many of our
experiments, we think that way.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:44.020
Now, if you think about it, though, when we
design a clinical trial, it's actually, in

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:45.350
some sense, the exact opposite.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:48.000
A
clinical trial, we explicitly lay out the

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:53.000
protocol, and the analysis, and the sample
size, and the selection criteria, and

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.700
all that, kind of explicitly ahead of time.

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:58.740
And data is collected with that in mind.

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:04.270
Now, of course, that's a very rigid and painful
process of doing that.

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:09.690
It's not conducive to more experimental,
discovery-based innovation.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:12.580
But there's maybe some lessons to be learned
from that.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:17.070
So my point is, as we think
about reproducibility , the reproducibility

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:22.460
is not something we tack on at the end when
we're ready to publish.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:28.440
Reproducibility is something that has to imbue
the entire experimental process, whether those

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:32.100
experiments
involve growing cells, or whether those experiments

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.120
involve writing code.

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:37.370
We need to be thinking about
reproducibility throughout the entire life

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:39.360
cycle of our data.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:44.160
That's everything from the initial acquisition,
whether that's experimental acquisition or

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:50.550
that's acquisition by making
simulated data, that has to be applied at

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:55.680
every step in the process, that involves computing
an analysis.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.290
And then
ultimately, at the end of the day, that also

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:03.480
involves thinking about, at the point of publication,
how do we promote

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:06.570
reproducibility at that point there?

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:12.320
So that's kind of a little bit about maybe
the philosophical framework in which

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:14.400
we're starting with.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.400
So how can we bring that to bear?

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:21.440
And how does that impact us in our daily practice?

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:26.570
Well, I'd like to start-- I'm
going to get a little more abstract.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:36.760
But I'm going to split the conversation here
into-- actually I think I've said here-- two

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.000
things, process and product.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.280
So I would just like to review process a little
bit.

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:45.620
And then we'll talk about product.

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:50.140
So process is more about how we generate our
data product.

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:58.140
And then product is we'll talk about characteristics
of that actual data.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:01.330
So in preparing for this talk, I actually
wasn't aware of this paper.

00:16:01.330 --> 00:16:09.130
But I found this reference to a very nice
paper here, which I think the paper outlined

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:13.850
10 simple rules for
reproducible computational research.

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:17.450
And looking through these rules, it's actually
quite good.

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:25.340
And I think it behooves all of us to look
at this checklist, and think about it.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:32.780
And when you do your computational
work, when you're doing your big data analytics,

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:36.250
which parts of these things do you actually
do in your daily

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:37.250
practice?

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:42.620
If I have a little time towards the end, I'll
show you some tools that we build, and we

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:46.520
use in our daily
practice, that actually make it really simple

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.650
to follow most of these 10 rules.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:55.210
So that's kind of the work that we do, is
how do we make sure that everyone can easily

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:56.340
follow these rules?

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:59.870
But
even in the absence of tools, like the ones

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:05.410
that we built which I think are pretty spiffy,
but even in the absence of

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:08.049
these tools, these are lessons that you can
follow every day.

00:17:08.049 --> 00:17:11.100
And, again, maybe some of them are obvious.

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:16.120
And, hopefully, many of you who are listening
to this already do this.

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:20.569
And if you don't, you should think about,
which one of these things could I start doing

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:25.110
tomorrow because they'll impact my reproducibility?

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:31.050
So it's simple
things, like track of how you produced every

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:32.050
result.

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:35.210
So that could mean write it down.

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:37.520
That could mean create a spreadsheet.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:45.590
That could mean use provenance tracking tools,
like Python notebooks or

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:47.670
R notebooks.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:51.930
Those are, potentially, ways of keeping track
of how data was produced.

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.640
But then you also need to
be a little bit careful.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:59.320
Like, don't go back and rewrite cells previously.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:01.490
So you still need to kind of keep track of
things.

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:03.120
Avoid manual data
steps.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:11.440
Thing you do manually are hard to reproduce
and impose errors.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:14.550
Keep track of all the things that you use.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:18.559
External programs, keep track of the version
numbers that you generate

00:18:18.559 --> 00:18:19.720
the data with.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:25.780
And for things you develop yourself, please
use get Git Hub or some other version control

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:28.250
system to
keep track of what you're doing so you know

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:31.559
how your data was produced.

00:18:31.559 --> 00:18:33.100
Three is quite interesting.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:38.310
I think it was fairly widely published that
there was a bug discovered in one of the

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:42.510
standard FMRI analysis laboratories that was
producing wrong results.

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:45.070
So he bug was fixed.

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:51.240
But there is a large number of publications
that were created using the software model

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:55.820
for doing analysis, for
which there was a bug, for which we have no

00:18:55.820 --> 00:19:00.510
idea which papers are now invalid, because
we don't know the

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:04.330
version number that was used to produce those
results.

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:07.170
So that's an example of three.

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:11.480
Now, number five is going
to this whole life cycle thing.

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:13.740
Don't throw away the intermediate results.

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:14.740
Everything is important.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:20.260
Every step along the way is important.

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:21.540
Storage
is cheap these days.

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:23.660
So keep all the intermediate results.

00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:25.440
Don't just keep the final result.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:29.190
Six, I'm a little less interested in.

00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:32.540
If you're doing random things, you want to
know how your random number

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:34.220
generator was seated.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:37.080
Number seven, always keep your raw data.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:38.280
Don't just give us the plot.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:41.900
Give us the data
behind the plot, presumably with all the history

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:45.020
of how it was generated.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:47.690
Allow you to kind of zoom in and out of your
data.

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:51.190
That's number eight.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:20:01.630
And number nine is, if you analysis or text,
you always want to connect them to the underlying

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:02.850
results to the data.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:08.740
We're going to talk next about how one
might go about connecting textual statements

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:11.710
like publications to the underlying results.

00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:15.580
And then 10, we're also
going to talk about shortly, which is we need

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:19.390
to provide access to the scripts, runs, and
results, because

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:23.760
transparency is the key to reproducible science.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:29.730
So those are some things that impact process,
how do we go about producing our data?

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:32.420
Again, I think there's
some takeaway here.

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:37.540
If you're not checking off you know seven,
eight, or nine of these things in what you're

00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:40.540
doing
today, go back after this lecture.

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:44.990
And think about, what can I do to increase
the number of these things that I'm

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:45.990
doing?

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:53.750
So now, let's talk a little bit about the
product itself, and the data.

00:20:53.750 --> 00:20:55.900
What properties would we like the data to
have

00:20:55.900 --> 00:21:03.590
that come out of, for example, these 10 simple
rules, that are going to promote this notion

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:08.980
of reproducibility so that
we can find data, so that we know how it was

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:12.710
produced, so that we can have a chance of
regenerating the

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:19.230
method, and then so we can reuse that data
as the foundation to do new things?

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:25.791
Now, what's come out of kind of the community
of people who care about these things, every

00:21:25.791 --> 00:21:28.360
day they come in
and out, and think about data, and running

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:31.480
repositories curating data?

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:36.360
What's emerged actually out of an
organization called Force 11 is this notion

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.540
that data should have some basic properties.

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:43.970
And say that we want our
data to be findable, accessible, interoperable,

00:21:43.970 --> 00:21:45.520
and reusable.

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:51.179
And I'm going to go through each of these
things in more detail.

00:21:51.179 --> 00:21:55.000
But these are called the fair criteria, or
fair guiding

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:56.360
principles.

00:21:56.360 --> 00:22:00.030
So what we would look like is our data to
be fair.

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:07.300
Now, if you haven't heard of these fair principals,
you have now.

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:17.230
And you should be aware of the fact that these
are increasing traction across the two things

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:20.060
you probably do care about.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:25.400
One is that the NIH is increasingly
adopting and pushing fair principles, and

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:29.740
driving that in to the grants, and into the
repositories where you're

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:32.860
required to deposit your data when you're
done.

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:38.790
The other thing is that scientific journals
are increasingly concerned about reproducibility,

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:43.830
and imposing elements
of fair principles on the submissions to these

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:44.830
journals.

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:48.990
So in spite of the fact that it's the right
thing to do, there are

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:54.680
also kind of self-serving reasons why you
want your data to be fair, because you're

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:56.400
going to need it to get money.

00:22:56.400 --> 00:23:00.480
And you're going to need it to publish your
results.

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:04.559
And the reason why it's the right thing to
do is because those results you get are going

00:23:04.559 --> 00:23:10.490
to be higher impact, and
have a better chance of being correct.

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:18.730
And I'd also like to make the point that,
often when we talk about fair data,

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:25.420
it's a trap to think that, oh, that's just
something for the repository people to worry

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:26.420
about.

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:29.260
That's just something
that I need to worry about when I publish

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:30.260
my data.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:37.810
I think the benefit of fair data applies throughout
the process, from creating the data to the

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:41.070
point of publication.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:44.799
So
fair data helps us achieve the 10 simple rules.

00:23:44.799 --> 00:23:49.730
And if you remember back in the earlier slide,
lack of reproducibility

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:54.250
was due to me not being able to reproduce
my own results.

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:57.590
And so if we think about fair as something
that is good

00:23:57.590 --> 00:24:03.830
throughout the process, we're going to go
after that issue of, can I even reproduce

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:05.260
my own results?

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:09.390
So what's good for the general community at
the point of publication, at some level is

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:13.059
good for you as you're doing
computational science.

00:24:13.059 --> 00:24:15.540
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to drill
down.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:20.260
And we're going to look at what the
definition of fair actually means.

00:24:20.260 --> 00:24:26.880
And in order to understand-- so FAIR is written
by kind of this data curation, data library,

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:29.000
data management
community.

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:35.490
And I'm assuming most of the people on this
call aren't active participants in that community.

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:38.190
So
there's some jargon or some background stuff

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:43.140
that I think that you need to have a basic
understanding of in order

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:46.670
to actually read the FAIR principles and understand
them.

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:52.929
So if you'll indulge me for a few minutes,
what I'd like to do is just review some of

00:24:52.929 --> 00:24:57.710
these background concepts that
maybe you're familiar with maybe or not.

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:01.350
And if you're not familiar with them, you
don't have to be experts in this.

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:07.000
But you should be aware of these ideas, and
have a vague idea of what they mean, because

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:08.360
I think they're pretty
important.

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:13.770
And, again, they'll help you structure your
data methods so that they're going to be more

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:14.770
robust.

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:17.070
And they'll be
more oriented towards FAIR.

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:20.220
And you'll have better reproducibility of
results.

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:25.280
So the two concepts I'd like to review before
we dive into the FAIR principles are the notion

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:30.250
of metadata, and the
notion of persistent identifiers, because

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:33.549
these two concepts kind of permeate the FAIR
principles.

00:25:33.549 --> 00:25:36.520
So let's start
with metadata.

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:39.410
So I'm actually going to take a little step
back.

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:43.610
And, again, this is something that maybe you
do implicitly in your

00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:45.270
work.

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:50.590
It's always good to think about these ideas
explicitly, because they help you design better,

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:54.470
and have better
experimental design around computational experiments.

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:58.640
So I want to introduce, if you're not familiar
with this idea,

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:01.510
the notion of a data model.

00:26:01.510 --> 00:26:08.110
So a data model, kind of in the information
sciences literature, is something that, basically,

00:26:08.110 --> 00:26:11.429
describes the structure
of the data, and the relationship between

00:26:11.429 --> 00:26:14.040
big pieces of your data.

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:18.200
So a data model at a high level talks about
the

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:19.360
things we care about.

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:26.770
We care about samples, and measurements, and
essays, and images, and sequences.

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:28.780
Those are examples of things we care about.

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:32.610
And how in attributes of those things-- so
what are the attributes

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:36.360
that describe a sequencing experiment, for
example?

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:40.790
And then also, we often don't think about
this, but the

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:42.360
connection between them.

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:45.210
How is a sample related to observations on
that sample?

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.690
How does a simulation run relate to an input
data file?

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:50.390
So it's things.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.570
Sometimes we call those entities.

00:26:52.570 --> 00:26:56.260
It's attributes, or properties, of those things,
and relationships

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:57.940
between those things.

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:03.390
And so if we have this underlying notion of
a data model, which describes kind of the

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:09.090
structure of the information,
another term people often use is that's a

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:10.090
knowledge model.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:16.140
Which kind of represents the knowledge, the
connection, the structure, of what we care

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:17.230
about.

00:27:17.230 --> 00:27:22.429
So the term metadata, which I'm sure you've
all heard of, is

00:27:22.429 --> 00:27:26.510
really just-- so we often say metadata is
data about the data.

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:27.640
So what does that mean?

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:32.890
So it's a structured set of attributes that
describe our data.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:36.600
In other words, it's the data
model.

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:42.840
So the metadata is, basically, the data model
that describes the structure and contents

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:44.080
of our data.

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:47.670
So when we think about metadata, what we first
need to do is think about, what's our metadata?

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.560
What are the
things that I care about?

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:50.600
How do I describe them?

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:52.390
And how are they connected?

00:27:52.390 --> 00:28:00.000
And then I can take those descriptions, and
use that to both find data that I'm interested

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.750
in, and figure out what's
in there so I can reuse it.

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:07.669
Now, there are many different ways we can
create these models, and ways of notating

00:28:07.669 --> 00:28:09.640
these things.

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:12.270
So you might have heard or a lot about these
things.

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:18.010
So things like the entity relation model is
an example of a way we might describe this.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:23.170
Or the resource description
framework is another example.

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:30.350
So these are common tools used for creating
data models, which means that

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:33.929
these then become tools by which we create
metadata.

00:28:33.929 --> 00:28:36.190
So let me just give you a little example.

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:41.490
So this is an example from one of the repositories
that I'm responsible for.

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:43.240
So this FaceBase.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.680
So it's a repository for looking at a cranial
facial dysmorphia.

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.890
And it's fairly broad.

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:50.960
We've got imaging data.

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:53.830
We've got kind of more facial morphology.

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:57.600
We've got RNA sequence data, gene
expression data.

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:01.419
We've got micro CT, all kinds of things.

00:29:01.419 --> 00:29:06.390
So here on the left is a picture of the simplified
view of our data model.

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:09.020
And notice it's structured.

00:29:09.020 --> 00:29:13.380
So we have the idea of the project, which
is creating multiple datasets.

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:16.360
Each dataset might have a sample.

00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:19.280
For each sample, we have multiple assays.

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:23.790
Each assay could have one or
more files.

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:27.700
Or we could just have files that directly
attach to a data set.

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:29.240
So this is our data model.

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:33.850
If you look at the right hand side of the
picture, you see a slightly more detailed

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:34.850
view of
this.

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:39.510
So you'll notice we have now four types of
assays-- imaging, clinical, bioinformatic,

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:41.640
and enhancer assays.

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:43.770
We have lots of different types of files.

00:29:43.770 --> 00:29:49.720
So we can have band files, TIFF files, et
cetera.

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:53.010
So this is this notion of
data model.

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:57.800
So when we talk about metadata in this context,
essentially what we're doing now is for each

00:29:57.800 --> 00:30:01.260
of these
boxes, we have a set of properties or attributes

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:03.270
that describe these things.

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:05.110
And that now becomes our metadata.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:09.190
So for project, we might have something like
the project name.

00:30:09.190 --> 00:30:11.450
We might
have the principal investigator.

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:21.690
For file, we might have the data type, the
file format, the date the file was obtained,

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:23.600
the instrument it was obtained from.

00:30:23.600 --> 00:30:28.690
So that's an example, now, of our metadata
and data model.

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:35.169
So that's concept one that you should have
some familiarity and comfort with.

00:30:35.169 --> 00:30:39.310
The second concept I want to
introduce is the notion of an identifier.

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:44.410
And on the material I'm presenting now, there's
a number of papers that

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:46.960
are out there.

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:51.700
The material I'm showing now is from a very
nice kind of blog post called Bad Identifiers

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:55.750
of the
Potholes of the Information Superhighway.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:31:01.890
So an identifier in it's most basic is nothing
more than a name for someone.

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:10.460
So if I have something that I care
about, it's often useful and very important

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:13.820
to be able to name that thing in a convenient
way.

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:17.240
And there's lots of
different ways I can name things.

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:22.020
So it turns out, if you think about this for
a while, there are lots of reasons why you

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:24.039
might want to do that, and
characteristics I might want to have on that

00:31:24.039 --> 00:31:25.610
name.

00:31:25.610 --> 00:31:28.710
So this cartoon here gives you an example
of some of the

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:35.030
reasons why I might want a convenient name,
and some of the properties of that name.

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:39.679
And some of these maybe you've already run
across in your own.

00:31:39.679 --> 00:31:45.150
I'm sure probably everyone on this seminar
has

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:46.470
probably made up names.

00:31:46.470 --> 00:31:49.150
And they might be bad names.

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:51.309
Or they might be good names.

00:31:51.309 --> 00:31:53.919
So let's look at some of these properties.

00:31:53.919 --> 00:31:58.010
And this cartoon is basically showing the
use of the name from several

00:31:58.010 --> 00:31:59.010
perspectives.

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:05.260
So from the basic researcher, if I want to
reproduce something and you get different

00:32:05.260 --> 00:32:08.950
results, well,
maybe I've actually got the wrong physical

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:13.350
thing that I'm trying to reproduce the result
from.

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:16.070
Maybe the mouse has
got a different genotype.

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:18.460
Maybe I'm actually using a different antibody.

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:22.490
So how do I know that I actually used the
same stuff, or used the

00:32:22.490 --> 00:32:28.770
same program, or produced the same answers
that another investigator did?

00:32:28.770 --> 00:32:32.330
So having a robust name could help
that.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:37.210
If you've got two published paper letters,
and they're using the same dataset, and the

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:40.000
same genes, and the
same analysis, how do I know if I've used

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:41.000
that?

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:42.890
I've done an analysis with a standard piece
of software.

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:46.410
How do I
know if these two people who produced the

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:48.460
result actually used the same thing?

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:53.480
So naming is important for doing
that.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:55.910
We'll skip the [INAUDIBLE] dependent perspective.

00:32:55.910 --> 00:32:59.460
I don't think there's too many physicians
on this call.

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:01.920
But
there's plenty of bioinformatics researchers

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:02.920
here.

00:33:02.920 --> 00:33:06.210
So how many times do we all see broken links?

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:07.590
We say, oh, here's the name of this thing.

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:09.370
Here's the URL on the website.

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:12.010
We go to use it.

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:13.010
And it doesn't work
anymore.

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:20.220
I can tell you as the operator of repositories,
before we took over people were just embedding

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:24.929
links to the website
in their published papers.

00:33:24.929 --> 00:33:28.720
So you go to try to reference something that's
in this published paper.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:31.880
And you get a
can't find error.

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:38.070
Did they use the same protocol that I used?

00:33:38.070 --> 00:33:40.750
Was the same algorithm used?

00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:44.120
The same code used one when they
published the result?

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:50.080
And then, finally, this last thing, if things
are named, then I have a better chance of

00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:53.520
not only reusing them, but then
tracking that other people are using them.

00:33:53.520 --> 00:33:56.370
So we want to report results.

00:33:56.370 --> 00:34:00.710
So these are all reasons why we might
want to have a name.

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:03.290
So names can be tricky.

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:08.590
I mean, if we think through them, there can
be a lot of complexity.

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:11.869
We have to be really
kind of explicit as to what we intend the

00:34:11.869 --> 00:34:15.650
name to be used for, and how we create this
thing.

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:19.000
So here's a real world
example.

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:27.480
There is an ID 9606, which, depending on how
you're interpreting it could be everything

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:36.300
from the name of a
species, to a name of a bacteria, to a certain

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:37.300
chemical.

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:42.260
So all these things have been assigned ID
number 9606.

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:45.000
So that's not a very good name.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:51.230
If I assign this thing, and I say, use 9606,
and you don't even know what it's referring

00:34:51.230 --> 00:34:53.179
to.

00:34:53.179 --> 00:34:55.480
And this is a real world
example.

00:34:55.480 --> 00:35:02.380
And if you look at this paper that I referred
to, they go into more detail about this particular

00:35:02.380 --> 00:35:03.670
example.

00:35:03.670 --> 00:35:08.830
So what are some properties that we would
like names to have?

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:17.400
So, in general, we would like a name to be
globally unique so that one name refers to

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:19.160
one thing.

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:21.180
So this is this notion of globally unique.

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:24.160
And this is what we call an identifier.

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:29.210
So the name refers to just one thing.

00:35:29.210 --> 00:35:35.619
That name that I can use that name to
somehow find, or locate, or get my hands on

00:35:35.619 --> 00:35:36.619
that thing.

00:35:36.619 --> 00:35:41.160
So that's something you'll refer to as being
actionable.

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:47.410
And that would be great if that name always
worked at some level so that we could always

00:35:47.410 --> 00:35:52.410
use this name, and find
something out about the thing that we're referring

00:35:52.410 --> 00:35:53.410
to.

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:56.450
And the term we use for that is we call those
identifiers

00:35:56.450 --> 00:35:58.109
persisted or permanent.

00:35:58.109 --> 00:36:04.110
So one way to think of this in the physical
world is that we go to a library, and each

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:07.400
book
has an ISBN number.

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:11.420
And that ISBN number always refers to that
book.

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:14.230
And even if I can't get a hold of the book,
at least I can get

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:19.400
some information about that book, like where
in the shelf it will be located if it exists,

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:22.760
who the author was, when it
was published, things like that.

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:29.090
Now, you've probably in your practice seen
things like this thing in citations, or

00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:33.450
papers, or when you go this thing that says
DOI.

00:36:33.450 --> 00:36:36.570
That stands for Digital Object Identifier.

00:36:36.570 --> 00:36:39.870
So that's an example of an identifier that
has these properties.

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:42.840
So there's
this whole kind of structure and infrastructure

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:46.390
that's been built to make sure that those
DOIs are globally unique.

00:36:46.390 --> 00:36:48.380
They only refer to one thing.

00:36:48.380 --> 00:36:51.200
They're actionable, which is I can use that
DOI.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:55.790
I can plug it into a web browser, and get
that information about

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:57.000
what it's referring to.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:02.810
And there's this whole financial business
structure built up so that I'm guaranteed

00:37:02.810 --> 00:37:05.210
that a DOI
always works.

00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:11.180
So the idea is 100 years from now, I should
be able to resolve a DOI, and get back information

00:37:11.180 --> 00:37:17.730
about that data, or
that publication, even if that data or republication

00:37:17.730 --> 00:37:18.730
no longer exists.

00:37:18.730 --> 00:37:25.340
And DOIs are increasingly being used now not
just for publications, but for data as well.

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:27.440
So that's an example of an identifier.

00:37:27.440 --> 00:37:28.610
And here's just a picture.

00:37:28.610 --> 00:37:34.630
If we look at this, this is an example of
a citation.

00:37:34.630 --> 00:37:37.350
Hopefully, you can see my mouse right
here.

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:42.030
So if you see this thing here, that thing
that starts with HTTP://zfin.org/, then ZFIN,

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:46.510
colon, and all that stuff.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:53.859
So that is the example of a persistent identifier
that uniquely identifies a piece of data in

00:37:53.859 --> 00:37:56.460
the ZFIN data repository.

00:37:56.460 --> 00:38:00.869
And that's what's called actionable.

00:38:00.869 --> 00:38:03.610
So those are, I could type that into a web
browser.

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:09.119
And I will get back information about that
particular piece of

00:38:09.119 --> 00:38:10.119
data.

00:38:10.119 --> 00:38:13.910
And this other one here is another example
of a type of identifier.

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:21.040
So this is an identifier that's actually not
resolved, or turned into an action by ZFIN.

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:27.340
But it's by the California Digital Libraries
is actually resolving that.

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:29.910
So that's our background information.

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:31.980
So now we can look in a little more detail.

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:38.260
When we say something is clear,
what does that mean?

00:38:38.260 --> 00:38:41.040
So what does it mean for something could be
findable?

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:45.470
So for something to be findable, intuitively
that means we know something about it.

00:38:45.470 --> 00:38:46.750
We can locate it.

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:52.460
Out of this
vast sea of data, we need to be able to actually

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:54.710
discover which piece of data we're interested
in.

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:57.240
And so this is going to focus on two things.

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:00.450
One of which is we're going to have some metadata
that describes this

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:06.500
thing so that we know we've got something
that's interesting.

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:08.610
And we need to name that metadata.

00:39:08.610 --> 00:39:13.290
So we're going to name that are there with
a global unique persistent identifier.

00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:15.140
So I'm going to have an identifier.

00:39:15.140 --> 00:39:18.170
It's going to name a collection of metadata.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:24.090
That metadata, point two, is going to describe
what's in our data with

00:39:24.090 --> 00:39:29.750
rich metadata, and interpret rich metadata
to say I've got an interesting data model

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:32.690
that describes what I've got.

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:35.370
That metadata is then searchable.

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:36.370
So we've taken it.

00:39:36.370 --> 00:39:39.480
And we've registered it.

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:44.860
And so we can look for things, based
on characteristics of the data.

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:50.730
And then, finally, that the data itself has
a persistent globally unique identifier.

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:56.210
So we can name and refer to the
metadata that we used to describe the data.

00:39:56.210 --> 00:40:00.020
We can name and refer to that data, both globally,
and persistently,

00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:01.020
and uniquely.

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:07.359
And then we've got a rich model that allows
us to find things that we're interested in

00:40:07.359 --> 00:40:09.030
So that's the notion of findable.

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:13.290
So kind of takeaway lessons here is, as you're
creating data in intermediate

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:17.460
products and publications, think about, can
I give it a name that's unique?

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:19.510
Can I describe it with metadata?

00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:21.730
Should
I name the metadata?

00:40:21.730 --> 00:40:24.741
naming Should I be naming the data?

00:40:24.741 --> 00:40:27.540
How should I search for it?

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:31.910
And then you might want to think about findability
in the tools that you use everyday.

00:40:31.910 --> 00:40:33.410
So what is my metadata?

00:40:33.410 --> 00:40:34.990
Is it
the file name?

00:40:34.990 --> 00:40:36.570
Is it a spreadsheet?

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:42.300
Do I think about, explicitly, what attributes
I want to have for this thing so that I have

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:43.300
a model?

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:47.440
Or do I just kind of
make stuff up when I stick it in the file

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:48.440
name?

00:40:48.440 --> 00:40:51.450
So those are all things we can take away on
a daily basis.

00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:56.870
To be accessible, that has to do with, once
I've found it, can I get my hands on the numbers?

00:40:56.870 --> 00:40:59.460
Can I got my hands
on the stuff I'm interested in?

00:40:59.460 --> 00:41:04.080
And this speaks to this notion of open science,
and the notion that transparency is

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:06.540
good for reproducibility in science.

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:12.040
Of course, we all know that there are reasons
why not every piece of data can be accessible,

00:41:12.040 --> 00:41:17.490
for IP reasons, for
privacy reasons, for data use agreement reasons.

00:41:17.490 --> 00:41:22.890
But, in general, what we would like to say
is that at least the

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:28.360
metadata-- I'm talking about our results should
be retrievable.

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:31.770
Given the identifier for a collection of metadata,
I

00:41:31.770 --> 00:41:36.820
should be able to use a standardized protocol
to get my hands on that metadata.

00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:41.490
And these days, what that
means is I should be able to use the web protocols,

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:46.260
or HTTP, to retrieve the metadata in a way
that I can

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:47.820
interpret.

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:55.550
So that means it comes back not just in raw
text, but maybe structured using CSV format,

00:41:55.550 --> 00:41:58.970
or JSON, or something
like that.

00:41:58.970 --> 00:42:04.160
That the protocol is open, free, and universally
implementable-- so, again, that says something

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:05.859
like
HTTP.

00:42:05.859 --> 00:42:12.620
That the protocol allows for authentication
and authorization where necessary-- so that

00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:14.420
says we can
protect it.

00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:20.390
And that we separate the notion of accessibility
of the metadata from the data-- so we should

00:42:20.390 --> 00:42:23.170
be able to know
something about the existence of a piece of

00:42:23.170 --> 00:42:27.470
data, and some level of the characteristics
of that data, even if we

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:32.520
can't access the data or know it's available.

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:36.070
So if you think about, if I go to [INAUDIBLE],
I can search and discover

00:42:36.070 --> 00:42:45.550
what genotypes are available, even if I can't
get my hands on the genetic data.

00:42:45.550 --> 00:42:49.440
And then these other things say,
and I should be able to go through something

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:54.210
like an HTTP protocol, and query that catalog,
and find this stuff.

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:56.690
I
shouldn't hide it behind proprietary information.

00:42:56.690 --> 00:42:59.230
So, essentially, metadata should be free.

00:42:59.230 --> 00:43:02.000
It should be widely accessible.

00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:07.800
You should be able to get to it using
standard non-restricted protocols.

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:12.520
And then we can address the question as to
whether you get to the data or not.

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:21.410
To be interoperable, so we want the metadata
to be structured in a way that I can interpret

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:22.410
it.

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:26.740
And so what that
means is often, we're going to express metadata

00:43:26.740 --> 00:43:32.160
using standard representations in things that
you've probably

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:35.890
heard this ontology term, or vocabulary.

00:43:35.890 --> 00:43:39.470
So we're going to have standard ways of describing
the metadata, and

00:43:39.470 --> 00:43:42.280
the relationship between the pieces of the
metadata.

00:43:42.280 --> 00:43:45.930
We'll typically tend to use a formal way of
describing that.

00:43:45.930 --> 00:43:48.609
So
things like earlier, I talked about entity

00:43:48.609 --> 00:43:49.609
relation model.

00:43:49.609 --> 00:43:52.560
Or more commonly, we use this thing called
the resource

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:57.359
description framework, or RDF, as a way of
describing our metadata.

00:43:57.359 --> 00:44:03.590
And we might render that into lots of different
mechanisms maybe, or representations.

00:44:03.590 --> 00:44:06.390
Maybe you've heard of this
thing called JSON LD.

00:44:06.390 --> 00:44:09.720
So that would be an example of that.

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:13.560
And our metadata will refer, potentially,
to other

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:14.560
metadata.

00:44:14.560 --> 00:44:23.150
So this is this notion that we're not going
to have everything in one metadata record.

00:44:23.150 --> 00:44:26.240
We might refer off
to other pieces of metadata.

00:44:26.240 --> 00:44:31.890
And so this allows us to get a description
of a piece of data, or even the data itself,

00:44:31.890 --> 00:44:36.730
and to be able to interpret it so we know
if it's what we want, how it was created,

00:44:36.730 --> 00:44:38.560
where it came from.

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:42.390
And, lastly, we want our data to be reusable.

00:44:42.390 --> 00:44:47.250
So that means, first of all, we have to document
our data with

00:44:47.250 --> 00:44:54.869
enough kind of details that are correct so
that we know we've got what we're interested

00:44:54.869 --> 00:44:55.869
in.

00:44:55.869 --> 00:45:01.880
We would like a clear
statement of reuse properties of that data.

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:03.970
So this is this notion of uses license.

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:10.500
So for example, Creative Commons is an example
of a usage license.

00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:15.210
And so
you should declare that explicitly.

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:20.500
You want some history of how the metadata
and data were produced.

00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:22.170
So that's this notion of provenance.

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:24.820
So you
want to know what protocols or methods were

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:25.820
used.

00:45:25.820 --> 00:45:28.369
So you know if you should be able to use it.

00:45:28.369 --> 00:45:34.310
And then, lastly, some of these things that
there are community standards that are defined

00:45:34.310 --> 00:45:35.970
for these metadata
and data.

00:45:35.970 --> 00:45:38.440
And you should adhere to that.

00:45:38.440 --> 00:45:46.200
So, for example, geo, or encode define community
standards for RNC

00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:49.480
data in how you should describe your RNC experiments.

00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:52.950
So that's an example of the main relevant
community

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:54.710
standards for raw metadata.

00:45:54.710 --> 00:45:58.980
So these are the ideas behind fair data principles.

00:45:58.980 --> 00:46:06.680
And the low level details of this are maybe
not germane to you.

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:11.490
But I think it's worthwhile to keep these
ideas in the back of your mind as you're designing

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:17.830
your experiments and
designing your data, and adhere to them.

00:46:17.830 --> 00:46:22.070
So let me just very briefly give you an example
of how we've applied some of these things

00:46:22.070 --> 00:46:24.710
in the work we're doing
in our BD2K center.

00:46:24.710 --> 00:46:29.960
So some tools for playing fair, just to give
you a hint for how you can apply some of these

00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:31.670
things in practice.

00:46:31.670 --> 00:46:38.570
So within the BDDS Center of Excellence, we've
developed three tools that help promote fair

00:46:38.570 --> 00:46:40.000
data creation and
exchange.

00:46:40.000 --> 00:46:42.589
And I'll just very briefly run through them.

00:46:42.589 --> 00:46:53.960
So there are these things we call a Big Data
Bag, which promotes access and findability,

00:46:53.960 --> 00:46:57.650
this thing called a
research object, which defines a metadata

00:46:57.650 --> 00:47:02.820
model, and helps promote findability and interoperability,
and then

00:47:02.820 --> 00:47:08.660
something we call a minimal identifier, which
meets a bunch of these permanent ID requirements

00:47:08.660 --> 00:47:11.060
that show up in
fair documents.

00:47:11.060 --> 00:47:15.530
So I'll just go through this very briefly
to give you just an idea of how some simple

00:47:15.530 --> 00:47:17.690
things can help promote fair.

00:47:17.690 --> 00:47:20.580
So
we've created a packaging format, or a way

00:47:20.580 --> 00:47:27.210
of collecting a collection of related data,
and then describing that data

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:35.450
in ways that promote access and interoperability.

00:47:35.450 --> 00:47:43.760
So in some sense, a BD Bag is nothing more
than a fancy zip

00:47:43.760 --> 00:47:50.180
file, where we've enumerated the structure
and defined what needs to be in that zip file.

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:55.760
And one of the most important things we include
is what's called a manifest, which basically

00:47:55.760 --> 00:48:00.530
lists the set of files
you expect to see, and records their contents

00:48:00.530 --> 00:48:03.510
using checksums.

00:48:03.510 --> 00:48:08.510
So a manifest lists all the contents and
checksums.

00:48:08.510 --> 00:48:11.470
And then we've also designed this for big
data.

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:18.570
So it might include data that we don't actually
have available to us, but are referenced by

00:48:18.570 --> 00:48:19.910
identifiers.

00:48:19.910 --> 00:48:25.870
But we have
the ability to enumerate all of these potential

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:28.550
contents, and know exactly what should be
included in there.

00:48:28.550 --> 00:48:31.109
So this
gives us a convenient way of collecting up

00:48:31.109 --> 00:48:33.060
and exchanging data.

00:48:33.060 --> 00:48:37.960
We use something called research objects,
which are rich metadata.

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:39.960
So we include this.

00:48:39.960 --> 00:48:41.140
This is an example.

00:48:41.140 --> 00:48:45.010
A
research object is basically a metadata model

00:48:45.010 --> 00:48:49.900
in some vocabulary that's been designed to
support interoperability.

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:54.089
So this is an example of metadata using a
research object.

00:48:54.089 --> 00:48:58.550
And what we see here is that, for example,
this is

00:48:58.550 --> 00:49:05.000
defining a file type using the research object
form.

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:09.940
And so this is a format called JSON LB.

00:49:09.940 --> 00:49:16.260
And this is using a RDF
underlying metadata model.

00:49:16.260 --> 00:49:20.820
And then the last piece is we've created in
this structure that makes it really easy to

00:49:20.820 --> 00:49:25.760
create permanent identifiers
for any arbitrary piece of data or intermediate

00:49:25.760 --> 00:49:26.760
data.

00:49:26.760 --> 00:49:30.829
So rather than you having to go and talk to
somebody, and

00:49:30.829 --> 00:49:35.250
belong to an organization, and have somebody
make the DOI for you, which is like what we

00:49:35.250 --> 00:49:39.460
have to do do now,
we've leveraged something operated by the

00:49:39.460 --> 00:49:44.410
California Digital Library, and provided the
ability for you to just, at the

00:49:44.410 --> 00:49:50.250
spur of the moment, create a unique identifier
for a piece of data, and to associate enough

00:49:50.250 --> 00:49:55.690
metadata with it so you
know exactly what you're talking about, who

00:49:55.690 --> 00:49:57.890
created it, and when it was created.

00:49:57.890 --> 00:50:00.170
So now, you can resolve that.

00:50:00.170 --> 00:50:06.109
And this is an example of where you get back
in following the fair principles.

00:50:06.109 --> 00:50:07.950
We've
now taken this identifier.

00:50:07.950 --> 00:50:11.530
We've resolved this back to a specific set
of metadata.

00:50:11.530 --> 00:50:18.260
So let me just give you an example of how
we might bring this all together very quickly.

00:50:18.260 --> 00:50:23.680
So here's a little use case
that we've done with the encode data.

00:50:23.680 --> 00:50:27.690
So in this case, our primary data source isn't
code.

00:50:27.690 --> 00:50:31.210
We want to do some
computation on data that exists in the code

00:50:31.210 --> 00:50:32.760
repository.

00:50:32.760 --> 00:50:33.990
And you can go to encode.

00:50:33.990 --> 00:50:34.990
And you can search.

00:50:34.990 --> 00:50:36.800
And you can get back a list of things.

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:38.119
And then you can go.

00:50:38.119 --> 00:50:40.160
And
you can download each of these things and

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:46.350
store it in a local directory, and then hope
you've downloaded all the

00:50:46.350 --> 00:50:47.350
right files.

00:50:47.350 --> 00:50:50.950
Then if you want something else to reuse this
data, they have to go and re-download that

00:50:50.950 --> 00:50:53.180
same set of files.

00:50:53.180 --> 00:50:57.170
So
that, obviously, does not promote reproducibility.

00:50:57.170 --> 00:51:01.690
So applying some fair principles to that,
we have used our tools

00:51:01.690 --> 00:51:08.890
to basically take an encode search, and turn
that into one of our Bag structures.

00:51:08.890 --> 00:51:12.609
So now, basically, we've enumerated all the
results.

00:51:12.609 --> 00:51:16.339
We've captured metadata about all of the results.

00:51:16.339 --> 00:51:21.280
We've then
associated a unique name to this collection.

00:51:21.280 --> 00:51:26.640
And then we bind an identifier to that collection
which is actionable.

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:31.010
So now, I can use and exchange this identifier.

00:51:31.010 --> 00:51:34.760
I can go and retrieve meditate about that
collection.

00:51:34.760 --> 00:51:38.850
I can retrieve
that collection, and then use that to access

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:44.690
the data so that I know that I've accessed
exactly the right collection of

00:51:44.690 --> 00:51:47.450
data, in exactly the right version of data.

00:51:47.450 --> 00:51:52.180
So now, I have the ability to start to have
reproducibility by applying these

00:51:52.180 --> 00:51:58.730
FAIR principles using these tools to computational
experiments that I might want to do on encode.

00:51:58.730 --> 00:51:59.800
So I'm going to stop now.

00:51:59.800 --> 00:52:01.230
That gives us a few minutes for questions.

00:52:01.230 --> 00:52:02.910
So just to take away, that we should always
So I'm going to stop now.

00:52:02.910 --> 00:52:05.030
That gives us a few minutes for questions.

00:52:05.030 --> 00:52:12.349
So just to take away, that we should always
be concerned about reproducibility.

00:52:12.349 --> 00:52:17.310
It's something that should inform your data
practice.

00:52:17.310 --> 00:52:22.700
It's not that hard to introduce notions of
reproducibility into your analytic processes.

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:24.670
You should always use
version control.

00:52:24.670 --> 00:52:26.599
You should always write scripts.

00:52:26.599 --> 00:52:28.750
You should always keep your intermediate results.

00:52:28.750 --> 00:52:34.220
You should always think about how you're going
to identify things in a unique way.

00:52:34.220 --> 00:52:38.870
So we need to practice
computational processes that promote reproducibility.

00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:42.200
And we should keep in the back of our mind
that, even if it's

00:52:42.200 --> 00:52:49.339
just for ourselves or our immediate collaborators,
that we would always like our data to be findable,

00:52:49.339 --> 00:52:52.290
accessible,
interoperable, and reproducible.

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:55.200
It's not just something we should care about
when we publish.

00:52:55.200 --> 00:52:57.900
We should think about how we can do this every
day.

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:02.750
There are tools like the BD2K tools that help
you do that.

00:53:02.750 --> 00:53:07.090
It doesn't have to be hard.

00:53:07.090 --> 00:53:09.200
It's something that's just good to do.

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:13.420
And it's becoming increasingly something that
you're going to need for

00:53:13.420 --> 00:53:16.329
funding and publication.

00:53:16.329 --> 00:53:19.460
So whether you want to do it or not, you're
going to be forced to do it.

00:53:19.460 --> 00:53:22.010
So with that, I'm going to stop.

00:53:22.010 --> 00:53:23.790
There's just a few minutes for questions.

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:28.260
And thank you for your time, and
hopefully attention.

00:53:28.260 --> 00:53:32.230
Thank you Dr. Kesselman, for this great talk.

00:53:32.230 --> 00:53:36.410
So a couple questions, but first there's a
comment from the

00:53:36.410 --> 00:53:46.640
audience, which was that in the discussion
of fair and data management and the data processes,

00:53:46.640 --> 00:53:52.520
one of the sort
of underlooked resources that people often

00:53:52.520 --> 00:53:56.580
fail to recognize as a great resource for
helping with a lot of these

00:53:56.580 --> 00:53:59.890
processes are your librarian's.

00:53:59.890 --> 00:54:04.290
So I think librarians, particularly at academic
medical centers and other universities,

00:54:04.290 --> 00:54:11.270
are increasingly involved in the data management
processes of big data, and other sorts of

00:54:11.270 --> 00:54:12.720
data repositories.

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:17.500
So
for those in the audience, and looking at

00:54:17.500 --> 00:54:21.080
data management issues, I think going to your
librarian is certainly a

00:54:21.080 --> 00:54:26.520
good thing, because they can also provide
a lot of insight.

00:54:26.520 --> 00:54:31.150
So one question that has come up is one about
context.

00:54:31.150 --> 00:54:34.790
And the use of data in terms of both primary
data and

00:54:34.790 --> 00:54:36.210
secondary analysis.

00:54:36.210 --> 00:54:43.780
So you sort of emphasized this at the beginning
of your talk, with respect to describing what

00:54:43.780 --> 00:54:46.400
the data is, how to get to it, and the such.

00:54:46.400 --> 00:54:51.240
But I think one of the challenges, particularly
in the biomedical space, is we often have

00:54:51.240 --> 00:54:58.750
data sets where the data is
collected for primary use, such as maybe clinical

00:54:58.750 --> 00:55:00.460
care or something else.

00:55:00.460 --> 00:55:07.900
And then it's being accessed or analyzed
for secondary purposes.

00:55:07.900 --> 00:55:14.119
And trying to understand what the context
is around the original intent of the data

00:55:14.119 --> 00:55:15.460
is often
undocumented.

00:55:15.460 --> 00:55:22.590
Do you have any thoughts on how FAIR, and
the annotation processes, and all that, can

00:55:22.590 --> 00:55:25.390
sort of
be interwoven into that?

00:55:25.390 --> 00:55:28.520
Yeah, I think that's a good question.

00:55:28.520 --> 00:55:31.710
So secondary use is always a challenge.

00:55:31.710 --> 00:55:36.990
So there are a couple of ways you
can approach that.

00:55:36.990 --> 00:55:43.990
So one is to design-- depending on the recent
reproduction of the data, to design with

00:55:43.990 --> 00:55:45.490
secondary use in mind.

00:55:45.490 --> 00:55:51.470
And so for some things, like these repository
studies that I have, that is the primary motivation.

00:55:51.470 --> 00:55:54.010
So we can do that
work upfront.

00:55:54.010 --> 00:56:04.650
In terms of kind of serendipitous secondary
use, one would like to make the argument that

00:56:04.650 --> 00:56:09.290
if you're
doing FAIR for your own primary use, that

00:56:09.290 --> 00:56:13.410
that would allow, at some level of interpretation,
for secondary reuse.

00:56:13.410 --> 00:56:22.990
And if you are capturing that as part of your
just good data practices, then that will help

00:56:22.990 --> 00:56:24.670
facilitate re-usability.

00:56:24.670 --> 00:56:31.270
However, at the end of the day, there is this
problem, which is that it's got to be, as

00:56:31.270 --> 00:56:33.380
we say, buyer beware.

00:56:33.380 --> 00:56:35.922
So
there has to be enough documentation to determine

00:56:35.922 --> 00:56:40.490
whether the data is accurate enough, or valid
enough, for

00:56:40.490 --> 00:56:45.660
whatever secondary use you might want to apply
it to.

00:56:45.660 --> 00:56:47.069
So there's no magic bullet.

00:56:47.069 --> 00:56:51.900
Sometimes you need so much detail that it
makes secondary use really difficult.

00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:56.119
I think
if we improved our data hygiene as part of

00:56:56.119 --> 00:57:01.339
daily practice, I think the idea is that would
help facilitate secondary use

00:57:01.339 --> 00:57:02.339
of data.

00:57:02.339 --> 00:57:07.650
So it's always just a good idea to think about,
if there are two ways to do it, it would be

00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:11.090
better to do it the
way that might facilitate somebody to reuse

00:57:11.090 --> 00:57:12.140
this.

00:57:12.140 --> 00:57:16.620
And in any case, we still have the reproducibility
requirement.

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:20.380
So let's get second primary use of the data.

00:57:20.380 --> 00:57:25.470
Somebody needs to be able to reproduce your
analysis, reproduce your analytics, so that

00:57:25.470 --> 00:57:28.810
the results could be
validated.

00:57:28.810 --> 00:57:36.670
Great, so another question which has come
up is a bit more technical.

00:57:36.670 --> 00:57:40.960
So the question has to do with BD Bag.

00:57:40.960 --> 00:57:45.300
And
they're asking specifically, how does BD Bag

00:57:45.300 --> 00:57:48.880
differ maybe conceptually from what you might
have in your classic

00:57:48.880 --> 00:57:51.170
TARS file structure from Linux?

00:57:51.170 --> 00:57:56.660
And, basically, the idea with the manifests
that you have in TARS, and the

00:57:56.660 --> 00:58:03.940
association information that's linked into
the manifests, could you provide some clarification

00:58:03.940 --> 00:58:04.940
on that?

00:58:04.940 --> 00:58:09.660
Yeah, so there's a couple of things.

00:58:09.660 --> 00:58:14.540
One is the manifest includes checksums.

00:58:14.540 --> 00:58:20.120
Not every file that's in the bag has to
be included in the BD Bag.

00:58:20.120 --> 00:58:23.450
So we have the idea of embedded references.

00:58:23.450 --> 00:58:27.170
So that's to facilitate big data.

00:58:27.170 --> 00:58:31.020
There's a little more structuring information
about the file.

00:58:31.020 --> 00:58:34.750
So, for example, the
name of the manifest is explicit.

00:58:34.750 --> 00:58:40.650
It's actually one of the files in the bag.

00:58:40.650 --> 00:58:45.790
The other thing is there is an RNC emerging
for bag structure.

00:58:45.790 --> 00:58:46.790
It's not rocket science.

00:58:46.790 --> 00:58:47.790
It's pretty simple.

00:58:47.790 --> 00:58:49.339
It's just
saying, let's agree on some basic structure

00:58:49.339 --> 00:58:50.530
of the manifest file.

00:58:50.530 --> 00:58:52.230
What goes into the manifest?

00:58:52.230 --> 00:58:57.650
How the data should be organized within the
archive.

00:58:57.650 --> 00:59:01.630
And then let's build some
tooling that allows us to do things like generate

00:59:01.630 --> 00:59:05.540
the checksums, generate the proper manifest,
fetch the data that

00:59:05.540 --> 00:59:08.200
might be distributed, things like that.

00:59:08.200 --> 00:59:09.980
There is a paper.

00:59:09.980 --> 00:59:13.310
And there's a nice website with all these
things on it.

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:19.369
And if there's any questions on that, please
follow up with me.

00:59:19.369 --> 00:59:27.160
Great, and last question really quickly, are
you aware of any particular resources, or

00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:30.460
tools, to achieve FAIR?

00:59:30.460 --> 00:59:34.400
Right, so there are lots of tools.

00:59:34.400 --> 00:59:36.250
So FAIR itself is a set of principles.

00:59:36.250 --> 00:59:39.310
It's not an implementation guideline.

00:59:39.310 --> 00:59:44.670
So tools are some combination of policy.

00:59:44.670 --> 00:59:54.530
So, for example, there's the ISA toolset,
that allows you to create

00:59:54.530 --> 00:59:57.150
reusable data collections around investigations.

00:59:57.150 --> 01:00:04.569
There are the various tools and services that
are emerging for

01:00:04.569 --> 01:00:08.540
creating identifiers, like [INAUDIBLE] or
some of the other tools.

01:00:08.540 --> 01:00:14.510
There are tools for helping create and do
annotations around metadata.

01:00:14.510 --> 01:00:23.230
So I think there are things like Biocaddy,
which has been created in BD2K program, that

01:00:23.230 --> 01:00:28.230
defines interoperable data models, and search
indexes, things like

01:00:28.230 --> 01:00:30.180
that.

01:00:30.180 --> 01:00:32.000
So there's no one tool.

01:00:32.000 --> 01:00:38.040
I think it's awareness of kind of what goes
into it, the underlying principles, and then

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:44.220
being more explicit about your practice so
that you conform to these principles.

01:00:44.220 --> 01:00:48.121
And, again, that practice could be, I know
it's got to go in a repository because that's

01:00:48.121 --> 01:00:49.920
a condition of funding.

01:00:49.920 --> 01:00:53.680
Are
my repositories organized around FAIR?

01:00:53.680 --> 01:00:56.740
Or it's just like, I'm doing an investigation
in my lab.

01:00:56.740 --> 01:00:59.280
And this would be a
good thing for me to do.

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:07.220
So let me think about, what is my strategy
going to be for findability?

01:01:07.220 --> 01:01:11.079
And then what tools that are available could
I

01:01:11.079 --> 01:01:16.880
use within my experiment to make things findable,
other than maybe coming up with a fancy file

01:01:16.880 --> 01:01:20.410
name, or a
nested directory structure?

01:01:20.410 --> 01:01:23.670
So that's what I would say.

01:01:23.670 --> 01:01:25.461
So I think awareness is the first thing.

01:01:25.461 --> 01:01:28.010
And then select the things that are right.

01:01:28.010 --> 01:01:32.420
You don't need a sledgehammer
for everything you do.

01:01:32.420 --> 01:01:41.140
So you could do just lightweight stuff, just
being cognizant as you design your

01:01:41.140 --> 01:01:43.660
computational protocols.

01:01:43.660 --> 01:01:47.770
Great, so we're at 10:00 o'clock.

01:01:47.770 --> 01:01:52.310
I'd like to thank you again for this wonderful
presentation and some of the

01:01:52.310 --> 01:01:53.310
discussion.

01:01:53.310 --> 01:01:54.310
So thank you very much.

01:01:54.310 --> 01:01:57.000
Thank you all for your attention.

